  "slug": "attenuator-monolayer-mose2-b6a2",
  "summary": "Recent preprints show that interfacial layers, from thermal annealing to monolayer MoSe2, measurably alter spin and terahertz response.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the role of an interface between a functional spin or optical layer and an adjacent organic, molecular, or two-dimensional layer in governing transport and optical response. In NDI-based organic spin valves with Co/Au or graphene/Co/Au electrodes, in situ thermal annealing degrades magnetoresistance, pointing to thermally driven reorganization at the interface as a controlling factor for charge and spin transport. Monolayer MoSe2 covering Py/Au or Py/Pt spintronic terahertz emitters attenuates the emitted amplitude about fourfold, attributed to absorption by free carriers. Optical spectroscopy of non-planar spin-crossover molecules on monolayer transition-metal dichalcogenides shows efficient interlayer coupling via Förster energy transfer. The new element is the use of a monolayer TMD as an active overlayer that modulates spintronic terahertz emission, alongside evidence of efficient molecule-TMD interlayer coupling. The organic spin valve study does not share the same spectroscopic or terahertz setting, so the link is interfacial rather than mechanistic.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
